====================================================================================================
=== Inspection guide ===
====================================================================================================

  Files to open in Excel/LibreOffice for manual review:
    1. boundary_high_margin_wrong.csv   — start here for sarcasm
    2. boundary_class_1_2_disagreement.csv — sentiment-flip examples
    3. boundary_low_margin_wrong.csv    — model genuinely uncertain
    4. boundary_low_margin_correct.csv  — comparison set (right answers, low confidence)

  Useful columns to sort by:
    ens_margin            — confidence
    text_len_words        — short texts often more sarcastic
    n_components_agree    — disagreement among components hints at sarcasm

  What to look for to confirm sarcasm hypothesis:
    1. Text contains positive surface words (great, love, best, perfect, amazing)
    2. But true label is class 2 (negative)
    3. And/or text contains negation patterns or counter-narrative phrases
       ('but the X broke', 'except when', 'until I tried to')

  Send back a summary: is sarcasm visible in 30%+ of high_margin_wrong cases?
  If yes → Stage 2 has signal to extract.
  If no  → revisit the boundary definition before building Stage 2.
